    {
      "id": "dec6724d408a6bd6c7b309133af646831e2dfef6",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2795461061,
          "type": null,
          "body": "In case anybody's curious:\n\nThe underlying problem was in XNU's BPF capture mechanism code; to quote a comment in the workaround I checked into tcpdump.org's libpcap for it:\n\n```\n                 * XXX - Mac OS X 10.6 mishandles BIOCSRTIMEOUT in\n                 * 64-bit userland - it takes, as an argument, a\n                 * \"struct BPF_TIMEVAL\", which has 32-bit tv_sec\n                 * and tv_usec, rather than a \"struct timeval\".\n```\n\nThat glitch was fixed in the kernel in 10.7; it handled the 32-bit and 64-bit versions of those ioctls - the ioctl code changed because, macOS being a 4BSD derivative, the ioctl code includes a structure size - correctly for both sizes, having separate structure definitions that correspond to the 32-bit and 64-bit versions of the structure regardless of whether the kernel is 32-bit or 64-bit. (Yes, a 32-bit kernel can handle 64-bit userland binaries.)\n\nLater 10.6.x releases worked around the glitch in libpcap.\n\nSince we no longer support releases that far back - or even many releases *after* 10.7 - this is no longer needed in Wireshark, so removing it is the right thing to do (it looks as if it was partially removed in earlier commits, and this just removes a no-longer-used variable).",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-10-03T08:37:11.911Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-10-03T08:37:11.911Z",
